artifact.self_contained_problem:
For Population II giant stars (low-metallicity, low-mass evolved stars), develop a physically grounded and empirically calibrated description of the mass loss occurring during the Red Giant Branch (RGB) and Asymptotic Giant Branch (AGB) evolutionary phases. Specifically, determine:

(a) the characteristic timescales over which mass loss operates during the RGB and AGB stages;
(b) the underlying physical mechanisms that drive the mass loss (e.g., radiation pressure on dust, pulsation-driven winds, chromospheric activity, Alfvén waves, or other processes), and the conditions under which each mechanism dominates;
(c) a quantitative law expressing the instantaneous mass-loss rate \dot{M} as a function of fundamental stellar parameters — luminosity L, effective temperature T_{eff}, surface gravity g (equivalently mass M and radius R), and metallicity Z — that is justified from first principles rather than extrapolated from empirical fits calibrated on Population I bright giants (such as the Reimers prescription \dot{M} = -\eta\, L R / M with a free parameter \eta).

The goal is a prescription \dot{M}(L, T_{eff}, M, R, Z, t) suitable for direct incorporation into stellar evolution codes that (i) reproduces empirical constraints on Population II giants (e.g., horizontal-branch morphology in globular clusters, white-dwarf masses, AGB tip luminosities), (ii) reduces or removes the dependence on tunable efficiency parameters, and (iii) provides reliable extrapolation to metallicity and stellar-parameter regimes not covered by Population I calibrations.
